relation_count

Specifies the number of relations of the transaction.

relation_hdl_arr

Specifies an array of relation handles of the transaction. Its
size is equal to relation_count.

related_trans_arr

Specifies an array of related transactions of the
transaction. Its size is equal to relation_count.
The transaction is related to each transaction in
related_trans_arr by a corresponding relation in
relation_hdl_arr.

Transaction signals can traverse value changes in time-based fashion via the
ffrTimeBasedVCTrvsHdl class. Its usage is the same as ffrVCTrvsHdl.

Memory Traversal

Memory Traversal
Memories comprise two kinds of variables: memory range variable and memory variable.
Although the variable types are different, however the value changes traversal of a
memory variable is the same as the way you traverse value changes of normal signals.
A memory range variable consists of a pair of integers. It records the starting and closing
index of memory variables, which have value changes at a certain timestamp. The first
integer is the index of the beginning memory variable and the second is the index of the
closing memory variable. For example, to define a block of memory consisting of 4 words
of 8 bits each, specify the name of the memory range variable as follows:
my_mem[0:3]

Also, specify the names of the memory variables as follows:
my_mem[0]
my_mem[1]
my_mem[2]
my_mem[3]

Suppose the paired value changes of my_mem[0:3] at time 0 (xtag) are 0 and 2. It means
that only my_mem[0] to my_mem[2] has value changes at time 0 while my_mem[3] has no
value change.

This section describes the following code examples of FSDB Reader:

read_verilog.cpp
//
// Program Name: read_verilog.cpp
//
// Purpose: Demonstrate how to call FSDB reader APIs to access
// the value changes of Verilog type FSDB.
//
//
// NOVAS_FSDB is internally used in NOVAS
//
#ifdef NOVAS_FSDB
#undef NOVAS_FSDB
#include "ffrAPI.h"
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#ifndef FALSE
#define FALSE 0
#ifndef TRUE
#define TRUE 1
//
// The tree callback function, it is used to traverse design
// hierarchies.
//
static bool_T __MyTreeCB(fsdbTreeCBType cb_type,
void *client_data, void *tree_cb_data);
//
// dump scope definition
//
__DumpScope(fsdbTreeCBDataScope *scope);
//
//dump struct definition
//
__DumpStruct(fsdbTreeCBDataStructBegin* pStruct);
//
// dump var definition
//
__DumpVar(fsdbTreeCBDataVar *var);
__PrintTimeValChng(ffrVCTrvsHdl vc_trvs_hdl, fsdbTag64 *time,
byte_T *vc_ptr);
int
main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
if (2 != argc) {
fprintf(stderr, "usage: rv verilog_fsdb\n");
return FSDB_RC_FAILURE;
}
//
// check the file to see if it is an FSDB file or not.
//
if (FALSE == ffrObject::ffrIsFSDB(argv[1])) {
fprintf(stderr, "%s is not an FSDB file.\n", argv[1]);
return FSDB_RC_FAILURE;

}
//
// Open the FSDB file.
//
// From FSDB v2.0(Debussy 5.0), there are two APIs to open a
// FSDB file: ffrOpen() and ffrOpen2(). Both APIs take three
// parameters, the first one is the FSDB file name, the
// second one is a tree callback function written by
// application, the last one is the client data that
// application would like FSDB reader to pass it back in
// tree callback function.
//
// Open an FSDB file with ffrOpen(), the tree callback
// function is activated many times during open session;
// open an FSDB file with ffrOpen2(), the tree callback
// function is not activated during open session,
// application has to call an API called
// "ffrReadScopeVarTree()" to activate the tree callback
// function.
//
// In tree callback function, application can tell what the
// callback data is, based on the callback type. For example,
// if the callback type is scope(FFR_TREE_CBT_SCOPE), then
// application knows that it has to perform
// (fsdbTreeCBDataScope*) type case on the callback data so
// that it can read the scope definition.
//
ffrObject *fsdb_obj =
ffrObject::ffrOpen2(argv[1], __MyTreeCB, NULL);
if (NULL == fsdb_obj) {
fprintf(stderr, "ffrObject::ffrOpen() failed.\n");
exit(FSDB_RC_OBJECT_CREATION_FAILED);
}
if (FSDB_FT_VERILOG != fsdb_obj->ffrGetFileType()) {
fprintf(stderr, "%s is not verilog type FSDB, just
return.\n", argv[1]);
fsdb_obj->ffrClose();
return FSDB_RC_SUCCESS;
}
//
// Activate the tree callback function, read the design
// hierarchies. Application has to perform proper type case
// on tree callback data based on the callback type, then
// uses the type case structure view to access the wanted
// data.
//
fsdb_obj->ffrReadScopeVarTree();
//
// Each unique var is represented by a unique idcode in FSDB

// file, these idcodes are positive integer and continuous
// from the smallest to the biggest one. So the maximum
// idcode also means that how many unique variables are there
// in this FSDB file.
//
// Application can know the maximum var idcode by the
// following API:
//
//ffrGetMaxVarIdcode()
//
fsdbVarIdcode max_var_idcode = fsdb_obj->
ffrGetMaxVarIdcode();
//
// To load value changes of variables onto memory,
// application has to tell the FSDB reader about what
// variables it's interested in. Application selects the
// interested variables by giving their idcodes
